{
  "Comment": "Data-quality control plane (the canonical state list; later work wires a state to real logic but never adds one). validate-authorize -> inspect-source -> create-temp-tables -> start-profile-query -> [WaitForQuery -> CheckQuery -> Choice] -> byte-scan -> assemble-report -> drop-temp-tables -> RecordSuccess. NO ASL STATE DECODES AN ERROR CAUSE: States.StringToJson in a Pass state raises an uncatchable States.Runtime on any non-JSON Cause, which killed executions before they could notify. The raw $.error travels to RecordFailure and Python decodes it. TWO structural decisions live here and must not be retrofitted: (1) THE ATHENA POLL IS IN THIS STATE MACHINE, not inside a Lambda; (2) every Catch reaches DropTempTablesOnFailure BEFORE Fail. Each Task threads the running request object via Payload.$=$ + OutputPath=$.Payload.",
  "StartAt": "ValidateAuthorize",
  "States": {
    "ValidateAuthorize": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "First step. The Pipe forwards the raw SQS batch (a JSON ARRAY), so Payload.$=$ hands the array to the validator (it reads record[0]); OutputPath=$.Payload makes the validated request object the whole state. A failure here runs BEFORE the request identity exists, so its Catch (no ResultPath — the input is an array, not an object) routes to ValidateFailureSeed, which seeds null identity fields so the shared failure path is uniform.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${validate_authorize_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "Next": "ValidateFailureSeed"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "InspectSource"
    },
    "InspectSource": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "List + classify by extension, probe every file's header, write the S3 MANIFEST and pass its key onward. States pass a manifest POINTER, never an inline file list: Step Functions caps a state payload at 256 KB and States.DataLimitExceeded is thrown OUTSIDE any Catch — so an inline list would give the user NO notification at all, the worst failure mode in the pipeline.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${inspect_source_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "DropTempTablesOnFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "CreateTempTables"
    },
    "CreateTempTables": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "Create the temp external table over the SOURCE (+ for CSV, the single-column RAW-LINE table the ragged check needs). Temp tables live in the USER'S OWN database, which is exactly why every failure path below must reach DropTempTables.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${create_temp_tables_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "DropTempTablesOnFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "StartProfileQuery"
    },
    "StartProfileQuery": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "START the Athena profile query and return its QueryExecutionId — it does NOT wait. StartQueryExecution carries a ClientRequestToken (= requestId), or a Retry after a post-submission transient error would start a SECOND query.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${start_profile_query_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "DropTempTablesOnFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "WaitForQuery"
    },
    "WaitForQuery": {
      "Type": "Wait",
      "Comment": "THE POLL LIVES HERE, IN THE STATE MACHINE — not inside a Lambda. table-builder's execute-ddl polls Athena inside a 300 s Lambda, which is fine for a CREATE TABLE that returns in seconds and FATAL for a profiling query over 250 GB that runs for minutes to tens of minutes: Lambda's hard ceiling is 900 s. A Standard Step Functions execution runs for up to a year, so waiting here removes the time ceiling entirely. This is the whole reason the arc works; do not move this poll into a Lambda. Seconds MUST stay in step with dqcommon.config.DQ_QUERY_POLL_SECONDS.",
      "Seconds": 30,
      "Next": "CheckQuery"
    },
    "CheckQuery": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "One GetQueryExecution PER QUERY (profile + ragged + duplicates), returning a per-query block each plus an AGGREGATE queryStatus the Choice below branches on. The aggregate is computed in the verb, not in the Choice, and that is deliberate: `ragged` is null for JSON/Parquet, and a Choice rule reading $.ragged.status on a null raises States.Runtime — which a Choice CANNOT Catch, so the execution would die without reaching NotifyFailure and the user would be told nothing at all. Only the PROFILE query's FAILED/CANCELLED raises TerminalError; a failed ragged/duplicates degrades to not_computed and the run still succeeds.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${check_query_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "DropTempTablesOnFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "QueryComplete"
    },
    "QueryComplete": {
      "Type": "Choice",
      "Comment": "Loop back to WaitForQuery while ANY of the three queries is still QUEUED/RUNNING; otherwise proceed. CheckQuery collapses the three statuses into this one value (see its Comment for why the aggregation is not done here). There is no unbounded-loop risk: the account's DML query-timeout quota terminates a runaway query, which CheckQuery surfaces as a terminal query_timeout.",
      "Choices": [
        {
          "Variable": "$.queryStatus",
          "StringEquals": "RUNNING",
          "Next": "WaitForQuery"
        }
      ],
      "Default": "ByteScan"
    },
    "ByteScan": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "The ONE thing SQL cannot see: a bounded, SAMPLED raw-byte encoding check. Its budget is PER REQUEST (DQ_SCAN_TOTAL_BYTES, default 2 GiB), divided across the manifest's files — never per file.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${byte_scan_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "DropTempTablesOnFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "AssembleReport"
    },
    "AssembleReport": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "Join the Athena results + the byte scan + the cross-file column agreement (computed from inspect-source's per-file probe, NOT in SQL — an external table has one schema for the whole folder and would HIDE exactly that disagreement) into report.json.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${assemble_report_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "DropTempTablesOnFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "DropTempTables"
    },
    "DropTempTables": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "Success-path cleanup. Idempotent by design — it must tolerate being reached before any table was created. Touches GLUE ONLY (no s3:DeleteObject is granted anywhere in this arc), which is what makes it safe to reach from every path.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${drop_temp_tables_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "DropTempTablesOnFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "RecordSuccess"
    },
    "RecordSuccess": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "Shape the terminal SUCCESS outcome the notification producer reads. The field list is the summary payload, which is wider than the notification's own sentence and deliberately so: the rule promises a runs-index entry can be built from it, and that needs format and fileCount too. A NotifySuccess added later (the mirror of RecordFailure -> NotifyFailure) reads THIS state, so anything omitted here is a field the notification producer and the runs index would have to reopen the machine to get. Nothing durable is written by a terminal state — the report object is the artefact, so no future UI should be built expecting a success object in S3. THE IDENTITY CONTRACT: every field read here must EXIST as a key by now. ValidateAuthorize guarantees requestId/actor/location; AssembleReport guarantees verdict/hasApproximateFigures/hasIncompleteFigures/reportKey and threads the rest. A Pass state's JSONPath HARD-FAILS on an ABSENT key (States.Runtime) and a Pass state has NO Catch, so that failure escapes straight to ExecutionFailed, skipping NotifyFailure and leaving the user with no notification at all. A null VALUE is fine; a missing key is fatal. (Caught by the live smoke test.)",
      "Parameters": {
        "requestId.$": "$.requestId",
        "actor.$": "$.actor",
        "location.$": "$.location",
        "status": "completed",
        "format.$": "$.format",
        "fileCount.$": "$.fileCount",
        "verdict.$": "$.verdict",
        "hasApproximateFigures.$": "$.hasApproximateFigures",
        "hasIncompleteFigures.$": "$.hasIncompleteFigures",
        "reportKey.$": "$.reportKey",
        "topIssues.$": "$.topIssues",
        "completedAt.$": "$.completedAt"
      },
      "Next": "Succeeded"
    },
    "ValidateFailureSeed": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "A validate-authorize failure occurs before the request identity exists (its input was the raw SQS array), so its Catch replaced the state with the error object. Seed null identity fields + carry the error under $.error so the shared cleanup/RecordFailure path is uniform for every verb. `location` IS SEEDED HERE FOR THE SAME REASON requestId and actor are, and it is load-bearing: the report path added $.location to RecordFailure, and a Pass state hard-fails on an ABSENT key with an uncatchable States.Runtime — so without this line the ONE failure path that reaches RecordFailure without ever having a location (a malformed or unauthorized request) would die before NotifyFailure and tell the user nothing at all.",
      "Parameters": {
        "traceparent": null,
        "requestId": null,
        "actor": null,
        "location": null,
        "reportKey": null,
        "error.$": "$"
      },
      "Next": "DropTempTablesOnFailure"
    },
    "DropTempTablesOnFailure": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "THE CATCH ALWAYS REACHES CLEANUP. The failure-path twin of DropTempTables, invoking the SAME Lambda (a state can have only one Next, hence the twin). Without it, every failed request would LEAK a temp table into the user's own Glue database — visible in the Query workspace's schema rail and eventually colliding with their real table names. ResultPath parks the result on a scratch field so $.error (the original failure) survives for RecordFailure. Its OWN Catch goes straight to RecordFailure: cleanup is best-effort on the failure path and must NEVER mask the original error, and this is what stops the failure path looping back into itself.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${drop_temp_tables_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "ResultPath": "$.cleanup",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.cleanupError",
          "Next": "RecordFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "RecordFailure"
    },
    "RecordFailure": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "The single recorded-failure state every verb's Catch reaches (after cleanup). Shapes the structured outcome { requestId, actor, location, status:'failed', error } (identity and location are null for a pre-identity validate failure, which ValidateFailureSeed seeds precisely so this state's JSONPath finds the KEYS). Nothing durable is written here either — a failed check produces no report object, so no UI should be built expecting a failure artefact in S3. The notification producer reads this state, and it needs `location` to say WHICH check failed: a user who started three of them cannot act on 'your check failed'. IT CARRIES THE RAW ERROR OBJECT, NOT A DECODED reason: this used to read $.parsed.cause.errorMessage, produced by a ParseError Pass state running States.StringToJson($.error.Cause) — which raises an UNCATCHABLE States.Runtime whenever Cause is absent or is not JSON (States.Timeout, States.DataLimitExceeded, Lambda.Unknown; verified against the live service with TestState). The execution died right here, before NotifyFailure, on exactly the failures a user most needs to hear about. $.error is copied whole because a plain JSONPath copy of a key that always exists cannot fail; notify-failure decodes it in Python via cpcommon.errors.failure_reason, where any shape is safe. IT ALSO CARRIES $.reportKey, which is null until assemble-report fills it — and since assemble-report is followed only by DropTempTables, a NON-NULL reportKey on THIS state means exactly one thing: the check succeeded, the report was written, the user was already notified, and the only thing that failed afterwards was cleanup. notify-failure stays silent in that case rather than contradicting the success message it already sent. reportKey is seeded null by validate-authorize AND by ValidateFailureSeed for the same reason location is: a Pass state's JSONPath hard-fails on an ABSENT key. IT CARRIES THE FORWARDED REQUEST FROM THE CONTEXT OBJECT, $$.Execution.Input, not from the seed that reached it: a failure whose error REPLACED the state has no $.traceparent and no $.requestId left to read, and the envelope is the only surviving copy of the run — notify-failure parses the traceparent and the request id out of it in Python, which is the one place that value is reachable, since prising it out in ASL would need the banned States.StringToJson. Reading $$ here rather than threading it up through the seeds is also what keeps this to one key on one state per plane: the context object cannot be absent, so it is not a read any seed has to supply.",
      "Parameters": {
        "raw.$": "$$.Execution.Input",
        "traceparent.$": "$.traceparent",
        "requestId.$": "$.requestId",
        "actor.$": "$.actor",
        "location.$": "$.location",
        "reportKey.$": "$.reportKey",
        "status": "failed",
        "error.$": "$.error"
      },
      "Next": "NotifyFailure"
    },
    "NotifyFailure": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "Emit the friendly failure notification to the actor. PLACEHOLDER here — the notification producer wires the real handler. Its Catch goes straight to Failed: a missed notification must never change the recorded outcome, and the execution has already failed.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${notify_failure_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "ResultPath": "$.notified",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.notifyError",
          "Next": "Failed"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "Failed"
    },
    "Succeeded": {
      "Type": "Succeed"
    },
    "Failed": {
      "Type": "Fail",
      "Error": "DataQualityCheckFailed",
      "Cause": "The data-quality control plane recorded an unrecoverable error (see the RecordFailure state output for the structured { status:'failed', reason } outcome)."
    }
  }
}
